target/
node_modules/
dist/
# Prebuilt native binaries staged into platform packages at publish time
# (populated by .github/workflows/build-native.yml / publish.yml, never
# committed).
packages/core-*/bin/*
!packages/core-*/bin/.gitkeep
.superpowers/
*.duckdb
# Local runtime store roots (git/hot/cold-tier ledger + cursors + trajectory,
# written by `canon ingest`/`sync` and tests; derived from committed
# openspec/ + specs/ sources, regenerable, never committed).
#
# FOUR exceptions, all for the same reason: the record is not
# regenerable from any committed source because it is AUTHORED.
#
# s42 (`close-the-open-loops`) carved out `EvidenceRecord` — an
# attestation, which `canon-report`'s digest module treats as "the
# git-tracked, PR-reviewed tier". The single pre-s42 record had to be
# `git add -f`'d for exactly that reason; now that `canon evidence add`
# exists, every record it authors and `canon gate promote` commits
# belongs in git without a force-add.
#
# s43 (`findings-are-records`) carves out `Finding` on the identical
# argument, and it was found the identical way: the whole corpus this
# change exists to produce was written, promoted, and then silently NOT
# committed, because the rule admitted only the kind s42 happened to
# need. A review finding is a reviewer's own observation — no committed
# source regenerates it, and the derived counts in `.canon/REPORT.md`
# are drift-gated against a corpus that must therefore be in the repo.
#
# Subject records join them on the identical argument: `canon subject
# new`/`status` author them and no source regenerates them. Left local,
# `spec_coverage.scope` reads every subject-tagged scenario as naming a
# missing subject on any fresh checkout (CI included), so the gate's
# answer depended on whose laptop ran it.
#
# Review records join them in 0.13 (`spec_coverage.require_review`): a
# Review is a reviewer's attestation that no source regenerates, and with
# the policy on, a fresh checkout without them reads every scenario as
# unreviewed — CI included.
#
# Written as `ledger/*` + re-includes because a negation cannot
# resurrect a path whose PARENT directory is excluded. `_staging/` is
# still matched by the `*` line, so a STAGED (unpromoted) record stays
# local — only promoted records are tracked.
.canon/ledger/*
!.canon/ledger/kind=evidence_record/
!.canon/ledger/kind=finding/
!.canon/ledger/kind=review/
!.canon/ledger/kind=subject/
# The evidence artifact store (0.14): the bytes `canon evidence add
# --artifact/--report` bound, one file per sha256. Authored on the same
# argument as the ledger re-includes above -- no source regenerates a
# rewritten report's earlier bytes, and `canon gate check` reports a
# latest record whose bytes are gone as stale-evidence -- so it is
# tracked, and this negation keeps a broader rule from ever hiding it.
!.canon/artifacts/
.canon/learn/
.canon/r2/
.canon/ingest/
# Live run manifests written by `canon dispatch begin`/`end` (s8; s40
# added the task/parent binding). One file per dispatched run, local
# runtime state -- never committed, and not yet reconciled into the
# record store (s40 proposal's "does NOT do").
.canon/dispatch/
.canon/REPORT.md
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
# Python bytecode cache (scripts/ helpers, e.g. the release-safety checker).
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Local-only vendored reference copies; kept on disk, never tracked.
vendors/
# Astro build cache (packages/website).
.astro/
